---
import Terminal from "./Terminal.astro";
---

<section class="install" id="install">
  <div class="install-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Fig. 4 — Install</p>
    <h2>One binary. <em>No setup.</em></h2>
    <p>
      Download the binary for your machine from the releases (Linux and macOS, x64 and arm64) and
      put it on your <code>PATH</code>. Swap in <code>linux-arm64</code>, <code>darwin-x64</code> or{" "}
      <code>darwin-arm64</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then run <code>quench</code> with no arguments. It finds each harness home on its own, and only
      reads.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="install-steps">
    <Terminal title="install" class="term-plain">
      <span class="prompt">$</span> gh release download --repo JarenKempton/quench \{"\n"}    --pattern "quench-linux-x64" --output ~/.local/bin/quench{"\n"}<span class="prompt">$</span> chmod +x ~/.local/bin/quench{"\n"}<span class="prompt">$</span> quench --version</Terminal
    >
    <Terminal title="update" class="term-plain">
      <span class="d"># update in place; uses the GitHub CLI</span>{"\n"}<span class="prompt">$</span> quench update{"\n"}<span class="prompt">$</span> quench update --channel nightly</Terminal
    >
    <p class="note">
      <code>SHA256SUMS</code> in each release lists every binary's checksum. The macOS binaries are
      not signed; a copy downloaded with a browser needs <code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine quench</code>{" "}
      before it runs.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
